1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 2.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,450
2, Windows Admin Center.

3
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:09,700
So far you have managed each server at its
own console, with Server Manager or

4
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:11,150
SConfig.

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:16,200
Now you get one web page that manages many
servers at once.

6
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:22,450
In this video you will learn how the
gateway works and where you can install

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:23,900
it.

8
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:28,150
You will walk through the installer and
its certificate choice.

9
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:34,000
You will sign in, add servers, and handle
the extra steps a workgroup needs.

10
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:40,650
Then you will tour the tools for a server,
and finish with how to protect the

11
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,100
gateway.

12
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:47,550
Windows Admin Center is a free tool set
that runs in your browser.

13
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,800
Your browser, Edge or Chrome, talks HTTPS
to a gateway.

14
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,850
In our pod the gateway is SRV01.

15
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,900
The gateway then talks to each managed
server over WinRM, Windows Remote

16
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:03,350
Management, and runs PowerShell and WMI
commands there.

17
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,000
That gives you three big wins.

18
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:09,050
You install nothing on your own PC.

19
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:14,100
The managed servers need nothing extra,
because Windows Server already has WinRM.

20
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:19,950
And Server Core, which has no desktop
tools, finally gets a graphical tool from

21
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,400
afar.

22
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:25,250
You can install Windows Admin Center in
four ways.

23
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:32,300
On a Windows 11 PC, it is a local client:
just for you, at localhost port 6600.

24
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:38,150
On Windows Server it becomes a gateway
that many admins share, on port 443.

25
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:45,200
It can also go on a managed server, or on
a failover cluster that keeps it running.

26
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:52,250
In our pod we use a gateway on SRV01, so
the address is https colon slash slash

27
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,700
SRV01.

28
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:59,950
Two rules from Microsoft: never install it
on a domain controller, and use Edge or

29
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:01,400
Chrome.

30
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:04,050
The installer is a short wizard.

31
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:09,900
After the welcome page you accept the
license terms, and then you pick an

32
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,350
installation mode.

33
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,400
Express setup chooses the network access
and port for your operating system.

34
00:02:16,650 --> 00:02:20,650
On Windows Server, that means remote
access on port 443.

35
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:24,100
That is what you use in the lab.

36
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:29,950
Custom setup lets you choose every setting
yourself: local or remote access, the port

37
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,600
numbers, the certificate and its
thumbprint, the full domain name, trusted

38
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:37,250
hosts mode, and WinRM over HTTPS.

39
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:42,300
After that come the certificate, updates,
diagnostic data, and Ready to install.

40
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:45,750
Next the wizard asks for a TLS
certificate.

41
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,200
TLS is what puts the S in HTTPS.

42
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:53,050
It encrypts the traffic and proves the
server's identity.

43
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:54,900
You have two choices.

44
00:02:55,150 --> 00:03:00,350
In production, you use a certificate you
already installed, issued by a trusted

45
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:02,600
certificate authority for server
authentication.

46
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:07,250
In a lab, you let the installer generate a
self-signed certificate.

47
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:13,500
It works, but it expires after sixty days,
and browsers warn about it because no

48
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:15,350
authority vouches for it.

49
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:20,400
Then you choose update and diagnostic
settings, select Install, and finish with

50
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:22,650
Start Windows Admin Center selected.

51
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:28,100
Server Core has no wizard, so Microsoft
shows a silent install with PowerShell.

52
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:32,350
First list C colon Install to find the
installer's name.

53
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:37,000
Then run Start-Process with the argument
slash VERYSILENT and dash Wait.

54
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:41,250
It installs with the default settings and
shows no pages.

55
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,900
Next, Get-Service WindowsAdminCenter
should say Running.

56
00:03:44,150 --> 00:03:46,550
If it says Stopped, use Start-Service.

57
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,600
Last, Test-NetConnection to SRV01 on port
443.

58
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:53,450
TcpTestSucceeded True means the gateway is
listening for browsers.

59
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,900
You run both checks in the lab too.

60
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:01,550
Now open Edge and go to https colon slash
slash SRV01.

61
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:06,200
Because the certificate is self-signed,
Edge says Your connection isn't private.

62
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:12,450
In this lab that warning is expected, and
you trust the server on purpose, because

63
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:13,900
you built it.

64
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:16,550
Select Advanced, then Continue to srv01.

65
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,800
Never do that on a site you do not own.

66
00:04:21,050 --> 00:04:24,250
Next comes the Windows Admin Center
sign-in page.

67
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:30,500
In the pod you sign in as SRV01 backslash
Administrator with the password shown in

68
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:31,950
your NetLab pod.

69
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:36,200
If Edge asks you to select a certificate,
select OK.

70
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:40,050
After you sign in, you land on All
connections.

71
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,900
This is your list of everything the
gateway manages.

72
00:04:44,150 --> 00:04:50,550
The plus Add button adds a server, a
Windows PC, a cluster or an Azure VM.

73
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:55,600
Manage as sets which account the gateway
uses for a selected connection.

74
00:04:55,850 --> 00:05:00,250
And the Managing As column shows that
account for each row.

75
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,700
By default Windows Admin Center uses
single sign-on: it tries the account you

76
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:07,150
signed in with.

77
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:13,000
In a workgroup, that account only exists
on SRV01, so SRV-CORE needs its own

78
00:05:13,250 --> 00:05:14,450
credentials.

79
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:19,100
To add a server, select plus Add, then Add
under Servers.

80
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:22,950
On the Add one tab, type the server name.

81
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:29,600
Our pod has no DNS server until Learning
Plan 3, so type the IP address, 192.

82
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:31,050
168.

83
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:32,500
50.

84
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:33,950
21.

85
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:35,400
It always works.

86
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:36,850
Select Add.

87
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:38,300
Now the credentials.

88
00:05:38,550 --> 00:05:41,350
Select the connection and choose Manage
as.

89
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,600
Type the account as SRV-CORE backslash
Administrator and its password.

90
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:50,250
The computer name in front tells Windows
whose account it is.

91
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:55,700
Both servers have an Administrator, but in
a workgroup they are two different

92
00:05:55,950 --> 00:05:57,150
accounts.

93
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,800
There is one more workgroup rule.

94
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:05,250
Without a domain there is no Kerberos to
prove who a server is.

95
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:10,700
So WinRM only sends your credentials to
computers on a list called TrustedHosts.

96
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:16,550
The gateway is the one connecting, so the
list that matters is on SRV01.

97
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,200
First look at it with Get-Item.

98
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:25,450
Express setup lets Windows Admin Center
manage this list, so you may see a star.

99
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:30,500
If it is empty, use Set-Item with the name
and the address.

100
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:35,550
Careful: Set-Item replaces the whole list,
so save the old value first.

101
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:39,800
Select a server and it opens on the
Overview tool.

102
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:45,650
On the left is the Tools pane, with a
search box at the top.

103
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:49,500
Type a few letters to jump to a tool.

104
00:06:49,750 --> 00:06:54,950
Overview shows the computer name, the
operating system, memory, up time and live

105
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,600
charts for CPU, memory and network.

106
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:01,450
Its toolbar has Restart and Shutdown, so
be careful.

107
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:06,900
Further down the pane you find PowerShell,
which gives you a remote PowerShell

108
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:09,150
session right inside the browser.

109
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:13,400
The pane scrolls, and there are many more
tools below.

110
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:18,050
Here are the main tools Microsoft lists
for a server connection.

111
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:23,100
You will recognize many of them from the
Windows desktop: Certificates, Devices,

112
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:27,750
Events, Firewall, Local users and groups,
Registry, Services, Storage, Updates and

113
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,200
more.

114
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:35,450
The highlighted ones are the five you use
in the lab: Overview, Roles and features,

115
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,300
Services, Events and PowerShell.

116
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:43,950
Some tools only show up when the server
has that role, such as DNS, DHCP or

117
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:45,400
Virtual machines.

118
00:07:45,650 --> 00:07:50,850
And the gear in the top corner opens the
gateway settings, including Extensions.

119
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:55,900
Everything the gateway does, you can also
do with PowerShell over WinRM.

120
00:07:56,150 --> 00:08:00,150
First, Get-Credential asks once for
SRV-CORE backslash Administrator, and you

121
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:03,200
keep it in a variable called cred.

122
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:06,650
Install-WindowsFeature with dash
ComputerName and dash Credential installs

123
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:11,700
Windows Server Backup on SRV-CORE, just
like the Roles and features tool.

124
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:17,150
Then Invoke-Command runs any command on
the remote server, such as Get-Service for

125
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:20,600
the services or Get-WinEvent for the event
logs.

126
00:08:20,850 --> 00:08:26,050
That is really what the gateway does for
you, quietly, behind the scenes.

127
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:29,100
Windows Admin Center is built from
extensions.

128
00:08:29,350 --> 00:08:33,350
In fact every tool and every connection
type is one.

129
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:37,600
Under Settings, then Extensions, you see
available extensions, installed extensions

130
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:39,050
and feeds.

131
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:42,900
Select one and choose Install, and the
page reloads.

132
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:49,950
The default feed lives on the internet, so
in a closed network an admin adds a file

133
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:52,600
share or folder as a feed.

134
00:08:52,850 --> 00:08:58,450
Windows Admin Center can also run inside
the Azure portal, in preview, for Azure

135
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:00,300
VMs and Arc-enabled servers.

136
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:02,550
It needs no inbound port.

137
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:06,000
You meet Azure Arc in Server
Administration 2.

138
00:09:06,250 --> 00:09:11,850
A gateway is a high-value system, because
it can reach every server it manages.

139
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:15,300
Keep it patched and limit who signs in.

140
00:09:15,550 --> 00:09:19,950
Use a trusted certificate in production,
never the sixty-day self-signed one.

141
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:24,200
Windows Admin Center has two roles for its
own access.

142
00:09:24,450 --> 00:09:27,250
Gateway users can manage servers through
it.

143
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:32,700
Gateway administrators can also decide who
gets in and how they sign in.

144
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:35,350
But remember the two separate doors.

145
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:39,200
Getting into the gateway does not open the
servers.

146
00:09:39,450 --> 00:09:42,650
You still need administrator credentials
on each server.

147
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:44,100
Four common mistakes.

148
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:47,550
One: installing the gateway on a domain
controller.

149
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:53,400
That is not supported, so use a member
server or a Windows 11 PC.

150
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:58,050
Two: using the SRV01 Administrator account
for SRV-CORE in a workgroup.

151
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:01,500
Use the target's own account, SRV-CORE
backslash Administrator.

152
00:10:01,750 --> 00:10:05,350
Three: setting TrustedHosts and wiping out
the old list.

153
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:09,600
Read it first and save it, because
Set-Item replaces everything.

154
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:13,050
And four: keeping the self-signed
certificate in production.

155
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:17,700
It expires after sixty days and trains
people to ignore warnings.

156
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:19,550
Time to check yourself.

157
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:22,600
Imagine you run the gateway on SRV01.

158
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:28,450
First, which protocol does the gateway use
to manage SRV-CORE, and what must you

159
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:30,300
install on SRV-CORE first?

160
00:10:30,550 --> 00:10:34,150
Second, Edge shows a certificate warning
at https SRV01.

161
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:37,200
Why, and what fixes it for good?

162
00:10:37,450 --> 00:10:40,650
Third, adding SRV-CORE fails with your
SRV01 account.

163
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,500
What do you change?

164
00:10:42,750 --> 00:10:47,550
And fourth, a help desk worker can sign in
to the gateway.

165
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,800
Can they manage every server?

166
00:10:50,050 --> 00:10:54,450
Pause the video now, think about your
answers, and then continue.

167
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:56,300
Here are the answers.

168
00:10:56,550 --> 00:10:58,950
One: WinRM, running PowerShell and WMI.

169
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:04,000
You install nothing extra, because Windows
Server already has WinRM turned on.

170
00:11:04,250 --> 00:11:08,650
Two: the certificate is self-signed, so no
authority vouches for it.

171
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:12,900
A certificate from a trusted authority
fixes it for good.

172
00:11:13,150 --> 00:11:16,750
Three: use SRV-CORE backslash
Administrator, the target's own account,

173
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,400
and check TrustedHosts on the gateway.

174
00:11:19,650 --> 00:11:20,850
Four: no.

175
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:25,100
Access to the gateway is not access to the
servers.

176
00:11:25,350 --> 00:11:30,150
The worker still needs administrator
credentials on each server they want to

177
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,800
manage, and those come from each server,
not from the gateway.

178
00:11:35,050 --> 00:11:37,450
I am at the SRV01 console.

179
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:40,900
I run the installer from C colon Install.

180
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:45,550
I accept the terms, pick Express setup,
and a self-signed certificate.

181
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:47,800
Install, Finish, and Edge opens.

182
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:53,250
I type https SRV01, select Advanced and
Continue, and sign in as SRV01

183
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:54,700
Administrator.

184
00:11:54,950 --> 00:11:56,950
In PowerShell I read TrustedHosts.

185
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:00,400
A star means the gateway trusts all hosts.

186
00:12:00,650 --> 00:12:05,050
Back in the browser: plus Add, Servers,
and the address 192.

187
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:06,500
168.

188
00:12:06,750 --> 00:12:07,950
50.

189
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:09,400
21.

190
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:12,450
Then Manage as, with SRV-CORE backslash
Administrator.

191
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:14,300
Here is the Overview.

192
00:12:14,550 --> 00:12:18,150
In Roles and features I install Windows
Server Backup.

193
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:20,000
Services shows WinRM running.

194
00:12:20,250 --> 00:12:23,050
In the PowerShell tool, hostname answers
SRV-CORE.

195
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:29,300
To wrap up: your browser talks HTTPS to
the gateway, and the gateway talks WinRM

196
00:12:29,550 --> 00:12:30,750
to the servers.

197
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,200
On Windows Server the gateway uses port
443.

198
00:12:34,450 --> 00:12:38,850
A self-signed certificate is for labs only
and lasts sixty days.

199
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:43,500
In a workgroup, use the target's own
account and check TrustedHosts.

200
00:12:43,750 --> 00:12:48,550
Protect the gateway, and remember that
gateway access is not server access.

201
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:50,000
In Lab 2.

202
00:12:50,250 --> 00:12:55,850
2 you install the gateway, add SRV01 and
SRV-CORE, and manage SRV-CORE with five

203
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:57,300
tools.

204
00:12:57,550 --> 00:13:00,350
It takes about forty-five minutes,
practice only.

205
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:02,600
See you in the lab.
